Harnesses for other implementations

One small program per implementation. Each takes the same two arguments
as cmd/dump, prints the same field format, and calls that
implementation's own code to produce the values. Together with
cmd/check they are how the results in ../CONFORMANCE were produced.

    ENCODE=no DUMP=conformance/<impl>/<binary> cmd/check

ENCODE=no because none of them implements the -e direction. It is not
part of the wire format, only of the corpus.


Where to put the implementations

Clone each next to its harness, under the directory name given:

    rns.js           github.com/liamcottle/rns.js
    go-reticulum     github.com/svanichkin/go-reticulum
    Reticulum-Go     github.com/Quad4-Software/Reticulum-Go
    Reticulum-rs     github.com/BeechatNetworkSystemsLtd/Reticulum-rs
    rsReticulum      github.com/ratspeak/rsReticulum
    microReticulum   github.com/attermann/microReticulum
    reticulum        github.com/sgiath/reticulum

Reticulum-Go replaces five module paths with sibling checkouts that are
not in its own repository, so four of them are cloned too, under
conformance/src/Reticulum-Go-Projects/:

    msgpack bzip2 pbt tagparser    github.com/Quad4-Software/<name>

Two of the names above are Go and differ by a hyphen and a capital.
They are separate projects and share no code.

so that the result is

    conformance/src/rns.js/
    conformance/src/go-reticulum/
    ...

The commit each result was measured at is recorded in ../CONFORMANCE,
and the row is only reproducible at that commit.


What a harness may and may not do

The six rules are in ../doc/harness. What follows is what they mean for
the directory, and not a second copy of them.

../CONFORMANCE says per implementation which fields were called and
which were transcribed. Read the numbers with that in mind: a
transcribed field tests this directory as much as it tests the
implementation.

Five kinds open their expect with the input lines echoed back:
recipient_private and ratchet_private for encrypted, link_request with
signer_public or responder_private for the two link kinds,
proved_packet with signer_public for proof, and group_key for group.
Those are not transcription and are not a third case. They are lines
the harness was handed, printed back so that expect holds every byte of
raw; no implementation code produces them and none is credited with
them. They exist for the encode direction, which no harness runs.


One directory here is not a measurement

    example/dump.py   the smallest harness that produces a result:
                      test/identity, thirteen lines, no implementation
    example/vectors.yml
                      the workflow that runs it, to copy into another
                      repository as .github/workflows/vectors.yml
                      behind it. It is here because this is where
                      harnesses live and doc/harness sends a first
                      reader to it, and it is not a row in
                      ../CONFORMANCE because it measures nothing.

                      DUMP=conformance/example/dump.py ENCODE=no cmd/check


Building

    rns.js            npm install in conformance/src/rns.js,
                      then node conformance/rns.js/dump.js

    go-reticulum      go build in conformance/go-reticulum

    Reticulum-Go      go build in conformance/Reticulum-Go, after the
                      four sibling modules above are cloned. Its go.mod
                      replaces them by path; nothing is fetched for
                      them.

    Reticulum-rs      cargo build --release in conformance/Reticulum-rs
    rsReticulum       cargo build --release in conformance/rsReticulum

    reticulum         mix deps.get and mix compile in
                      conformance/src/reticulum, then run dump.exs with
                      every _build/dev/lib/*/ebin on the code path. A
                      runtime without root:
                          nix shell nixpkgs#elixir

    microReticulum    cmake the library first, then link the harness
                      against libmicroReticulum.a and libCryptoLib.a.
                      With GCC 16 the library needs -include cstdint:
                      src/microReticulum/Utilities/Memory.h uses uint8_t
                      and uint32_t without including <cstdint>, which
                      older compilers supplied transitively.

                      cmake -S . -B build -DRNS_BUILD_TESTS=OFF \
                          -DRNS_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF -DRNS_BUILD_INTEROP=OFF \
                          -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS="-include cstdint"

                      The harness needs the include paths of the
                      dependencies cmake fetched, under
                      build/_deps/*-src/{src,include}, and both src and
                      src/microReticulum: the headers include each
                      other unqualified.

Two of the three link kinds need more than the packet they describe.
linkproof and linkdata carry the link request as their first blob,
because the link id, and with it the salt of the key derivation, is in
no packet on the link. A harness that ignores that blob cannot produce
a correct field for either kind.

Six of the seven exit 77 for the same five kinds. The seventh,
Reticulum-Go, runs ifac as well, because its interface access codes sit
in a package that needs no interface; ../CONFORMANCE says what that
changes about its denominator. The other four kinds are still skipped
by everyone.
